---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Command from "../../components/Command.astro";
import Step from "../../components/Step.astro";
import { RELEASES_PAGE, formatSize, latest } from "../../lib/releases";

const release = await latest();
const url = release?.linux?.url ?? RELEASES_PAGE;
const app = "~/Applications/Nyanga-x86_64.AppImage";
// The .deb and .rpm only exist from the release that added them; until then the page skips them.
const deb = release?.deb;
const rpm = release?.rpm;
const packages = Boolean(deb && rpm);
---

<Docs
  title="Installing Nyanga on Linux"
  description="Run Nyanga, the cozy manga downloader, on Linux: the AppImage, or a .deb for Ubuntu and Debian or an .rpm for Fedora. Commands you can copy included."
  heading="On Linux"
  lead={`Most 64-bit Linux systems from the last few years${release?.linux ? ` · version ${release.version}` : ""}`}
  sections={[
    ...(packages ? [{ id: "which", title: "Which one?" }] : []),
    { id: "appimage", title: "The AppImage" },
    ...(packages ? [{ id: "deb", title: "Ubuntu, Debian, Mint" }, { id: "rpm", title: "Fedora, openSUSE" }] : []),
    { id: "updates", title: "Updates" },
    { id: "trouble", title: "If it won't start" },
    { id: "remove", title: "Removing it" },
  ]}
>
  {packages && (
    <>
      <h2 id="which">Which one?</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><b>The AppImage</b> is one file that runs on almost any Linux, with nothing to install. <b>The .deb or .rpm</b> installs Nyanga like your other apps, so it's in your app menu right away. Either way Nyanga updates itself, so pick whichever you like.</p>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="appimage">The AppImage</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <Step title="Download the AppImage">
      <p>Get it here: <a href={url}>Nyanga-x86_64.AppImage</a>{release?.linux && ` (${formatSize(release.linux.size)})`}. That one file is the whole app.</p>
    </Step>
    <Step title="Give it a home and let it run">
      <p>Move it somewhere it can stay, like an <b>Applications</b> folder in your home, and allow it to run. In most file managers that's right-click → <b>Properties</b> → <b>Permissions</b>, then tick <b>Allow executing file as program</b> (KDE calls it <b>Is executable</b>). Or paste this into a terminal, which does all of it:</p>
      <Command code={`mkdir -p ~/Applications\nmv ~/Downloads/Nyanga-x86_64.AppImage ~/Applications/\nchmod +x ${app}`} />
    </Step>
    <Step title="Open it and add it to your menu">
      <p>Double-click it, or start it from a terminal:</p>
      <Command code={app} />
      <p>Then in Nyanga's <b>Settings</b>, press <b>Add Nyanga to your app menu</b>. From then on it sits with your other apps ✦</p>
    </Step>
  </ol>

  {packages && deb && rpm && (
    <>
      <h2 id="deb">Ubuntu, Debian, Mint</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <Step title="Download the .deb">
          <p>Get it here: <a href={deb.url}>{deb.name}</a> ({formatSize(deb.size)}).</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Install it">
          <p>Double-click it to open it in your software center and press <b>Install</b>, or paste this into a terminal:</p>
          <Command code={`sudo apt install ~/Downloads/${deb.name}`} />
        </Step>
        <Step title="Open it">
          <p>Find <b>Nyanga</b> in your app menu ✦</p>
        </Step>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="rpm">Fedora, openSUSE</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <Step title="Download the .rpm">
          <p>Get it here: <a href={rpm.url}>{rpm.name}</a> ({formatSize(rpm.size)}).</p>
        </Step>
        <Step title="Install it">
          <p>Double-click it to open it in your software center and press <b>Install</b>, or paste the line for your system into a terminal:</p>
          <Command code={`sudo dnf install ~/Downloads/${rpm.name}`} label="Fedora" />
          <Command code={`sudo zypper install ~/Downloads/${rpm.name}`} label="openSUSE" />
        </Step>
        <Step title="Open it">
          <p>Find <b>Nyanga</b> in your app menu ✦</p>
        </Step>
      </ol>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
  <div class="box">
    <p>When there's a new version, Nyanga shows a little card. Press <b>Update now</b> and it updates itself and opens again, no password needed.</p>
    <p>The AppImage swaps in the new file, so keep it somewhere in your home. {packages && "The .deb and .rpm keep their updates in your own folder, so your system's updater will keep showing the version you first installed. That's fine: Nyanga always opens the newest one."}</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="trouble">If it won't start</h2>
  <div class="box">
    <p>A few systems can't mount AppImages (you'll see a message about <b>FUSE</b>). This runs it anyway:</p>
    <Command code={`${app} --appimage-extract-and-run`} />
  </div>

  <h2 id="remove">Removing it</h2>
  <div class="box">
    <p><b>The AppImage:</b> in Nyanga's <b>Settings</b>, take it out of the app menu, then delete the file.</p>
    {packages && (
      <>
        <p><b>The .deb or .rpm:</b> remove it like any other app:</p>
        <Command code="sudo apt remove nyanga" label="Ubuntu, Debian, Mint" />
        <Command code="sudo dnf remove nyanga" label="Fedora" />
      </>
    )}
    <p>Your downloaded manga stay where they are. To remove your library, settings{packages && " and Nyanga's updates"} too:</p>
    <Command code="rm -r ~/.local/share/Nyanga ~/.config/Nyanga" />
  </div>
</Docs>
